# Copy to `.env` and fill in the values before running `npm run dev` or `docker compose up`.

## Server

PORT=3000
HOST_BIND=0.0.0.0
# Set to `1` behind a trusted reverse proxy so secure cookies and client IPs are handled correctly.
# Leave unset for local development without a proxy. The Docker image enables it by default.
# TRUST_PROXY=1
# How many proxies in front of the server append to X-Forwarded-For (only read with TRUST_PROXY=1).
# The client address is the entry that many places from the right, so 1 fits a single proxy such as
# Traefik; use 2 when a CDN or another proxy sits ahead of it. Too low trusts an address the client
# wrote, which defeats the per-IP limits; too high makes every caller look like the first proxy.
# TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=1
# Live socket connections the whole instance accepts (connections waiting to reconnect do not count).
# A memory guard; the per-account and per-address caps below keep a few people from filling it.
MAX_PARTICIPANTS=500
# Maximum server membership. This only blocks new joins; existing members are not removed.
MAX_SERVER_MEMBERS=100
# Maximum Socket.IO payload size for signaling and chat; file uploads are unaffected.
MAX_MSG_BYTES=65536
# Reconnect grace period before a dropped connection counts as leaving the room.
RECONNECT_GRACE_MS=30000
# The same for a connection that was in a call. Longer so that a phone that suspends the page in the background (Android) finds the call still there when it comes back.
CALL_RECONNECT_GRACE_MS=180000
# After a server restart (a deploy), how long people who were in a call are held for their clients to reconnect.
CALL_RESTORE_GRACE_MS=90000

## Database (PostgreSQL)

DATABASE_URL=postgres://usuario:senha@localhost:5432/linkord
# Set to `1` when PostgreSQL requires or supports TLS.
DATABASE_SSL=0
# Required only for a self-signed certificate or private CA. Provide the trusted PEM certificate
# on one line, escaping line breaks as `\n`. Certificate verification cannot be disabled; an
# untrusted TLS connection fails instead of exposing database credentials to interception.
# DATABASE_SSL_CA="-----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----\n...\n-----END CERTIFICATE-----"
# Apply pending migrations at startup; set to `0` to run them manually.
MIGRATE_ON_BOOT=1

## Accounts and sessions

# Registration is disabled by default. Set a code to allow new accounts.
REGISTRATION_CODE=
# Deprecated: registering with this username no longer grants administrator access.
# Use `npm run admin:grant -- <username>` instead.
# ADMIN_USERNAME=
SESSION_COOKIE=ss_session
SESSION_TTL_DAYS=30
# A session unused for this many days ends even before its TTL (0 turns it off). An open tab counts as use.
SESSION_IDLE_DAYS=14
# `auto` uses `X-Forwarded-Proto` behind a proxy. Set `1` or `0` explicitly for local development.
COOKIE_SECURE=auto

# Resend credentials for the codes sent by email: account recovery, email changes and two-step login.
# Without them two-step login cannot be turned on; accounts that already use it cannot sign in until they are back.
RESEND_API_KEY=
RESEND_FROM_EMAIL=Linkord <noreply@your-domain.com>
# Only the end-to-end stack changes this (to its local mail sink); leave it unset.
# RESEND_API_URL=https://api.resend.com
APP_URL=http://localhost:5173

# Generate a Web Push key pair with `npx web-push generate-vapid-keys` and reuse it across deployments.
# In production, `APP_URL` must use HTTPS. Keep the private key on the server; never expose it to the frontend.
PUSH_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
PUSH_VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
# Example subject: mailto:admin@example.com
PUSH_VAPID_SUBJECT=

## LiveKit (voice, video, and screen sharing) — https://cloud.livekit.io
# For local development run LiveKit's dev server in Docker instead (see "Voice and video locally" in docs/DEVELOPMENT.md)
# and set LIVEKIT_URL=ws://localhost:7880, LIVEKIT_API_KEY=devkey, LIVEKIT_API_SECRET=secret.
LIVEKIT_URL=wss://your-project.livekit.cloud
LIVEKIT_API_KEY=
LIVEKIT_API_SECRET=
# Prefix used to create one LiveKit room per voice channel.
LIVEKIT_ROOM_NAME=linkord-room
# Room token lifetime in seconds. Existing connections are unaffected.
LIVEKIT_TOKEN_TTL_SECONDS=300

## Attachments

# Directory for uploaded files. The default is `<repo>/uploads` in development and `/app/uploads`
# in Docker, matching the volume in `docker-compose.yml`. Set only to use a different path.
# UPLOAD_DIR=./uploads

# Where "Download my data" keeps the ZIPs it builds until they are downloaded or expire (7 days).
# The default is `exports/` inside UPLOAD_DIR, so the uploads volume already persists them; backups
# leave that folder out. Set only to use another path, which the app must be able to write.
# EXPORT_DIR=./uploads/exports

# Largest single file anyone may upload, in bytes (default 512 MB). The composer learns it from the
# server, so changing it needs no rebuild. It also sets how much one account may hold in uploads
# not sent yet (4 times this, one full message); files already sent have no per-account quota, only
# the instance-wide storage total. See "Upload limits" in docs/DEVELOPMENT.md.
# MAX_ATTACHMENT_BYTES=536870912

# Optional for local development when sharing a database with another instance: attachment records
# are shared, but files remain on the instance that received them. Missing files are fetched and
# cached from this URL. Do not set it on the instance that stores the files.
# UPLOADS_REMOTE_URL=https://your-instance.example.com

## Backups (`npm run backup`, `npm run restore`; see "Backup and restore" in docs/DEVELOPMENT.md)

# Where backups are written, one dated folder each. The default is `<repo>/backups` in development and
# `/app/backups` in Docker, matching the volume in `docker-compose.yml`. It cannot be inside UPLOAD_DIR.
# BACKUP_DIR=./backups
# How many of the newest backups rotation keeps; a whole number, at least 1.
# BACKUP_KEEP=7
# Only for the optional `backup` compose service: seconds between backups.
# BACKUP_INTERVAL_SECONDS=86400
# Path of the PostgreSQL client tools when they are not on the PATH (the Docker image has them).
# PG_DUMP_BIN=pg_dump
# PG_RESTORE_BIN=pg_restore

## Abuse limits and quotas (initial values; tune based on actual usage)

# REGISTRATIONS_PER_IP_PER_HOUR=5
# MAX_NEW_ACCOUNTS_PER_HOUR=30
# MAX_CONNECTIONS_PER_USER=4
# Live connections from one address, all accounts together (needs TRUST_PROXY=1 behind a proxy).
# MAX_CONNECTIONS_PER_IP=20
# MAX_OWNED_SERVERS_PER_USER=20
# MAX_SERVER_MEMBERSHIPS_PER_USER=100
# MAX_CHANNELS_PER_SERVER=50
# MAX_ROLES_PER_SERVER=25
# MAX_INVITES_PER_SERVER=50
# MAX_WEBHOOKS_PER_CHANNEL=10
# MAX_FRIENDS=500
# MAX_PENDING_OUTGOING_REQUESTS=50
# Additional browser origins allowed for POST/PUT/DELETE requests and Socket.IO, comma-separated.
# ALLOWED_ORIGINS=
# Set to `1` to send the Content-Security-Policy as Report-Only: violations are logged, nothing is blocked.
# For trying out a policy change; see "Content-Security-Policy" in docs/DEVELOPMENT.md.
# CSP_REPORT_ONLY=1

## Orphaned file cleanup

# The daily sweep reports files missing from the database by default. Set to `0` to delete them;
# an administrator can also run a cleanup from `/admin/system`.
# ORPHAN_SWEEP_DRY_RUN=1

## Data retention
# How many days each kind of record is kept; a daily sweep deletes older ones in small batches and
# the result shows in `/admin/system`. `0` keeps that kind forever. A value that is not a whole
# number of days stops the server at boot. The audit trail and migration repairs are evidence, so
# they are kept unless you set a limit. Each server's own audit log (shown in its settings) keeps 90
# days by default.
# AUDIT_RETENTION_DAYS=0
# DATA_REPAIRS_RETENTION_DAYS=0
# NOTIFICATION_READ_RETENTION_DAYS=30
# NOTIFICATION_UNREAD_RETENTION_DAYS=90
# OUTBOX_RETENTION_DAYS=7
# SERVER_AUDIT_RETENTION_DAYS=90

## KLIPY (GIFs) — get the key from the KLIPY Partner Panel
# The browser calls KLIPY directly, as required by its terms. An empty key hides the GIF button.
KLIPY_API_KEY=

## MyAnimeList — register an app at https://myanimelist.net/apiconfig to get the client ID
# Public lists are read with the official API using only this ID. An empty value leaves MyAnimeList connections unavailable.
MAL_CLIENT_ID=

## IGDB (game search for the Wall's games widgets) — register an app at https://dev.twitch.tv/console (the Twitch account needs 2FA)
# Free for non-commercial use under the Twitch Developer Service Agreement. The server signs in with a client-credentials
# token and proxies the search, so these never reach the browser. If either is empty, searching for games answers
# "unavailable"; lists already saved still show.
IGDB_CLIENT_ID=
IGDB_CLIENT_SECRET=

## Media proxy — covers, GitHub avatars, link preview pictures and YouTube thumbnails served from our own domain
# On by default: they then show on networks that block those services, which never see the viewer's IP. It costs
# outbound bandwidth and up to 64 MB of memory for the cache. 0 saves that: the browser is redirected to each service.
# MEDIA_PROXY=1
